# Text this rule governs is written to the index as LF, so a fresh checkout is LF
# on every platform. There are three exceptions in the tree today, and the rule
# cannot remove them — it governs what is *written*, not bytes already in a blob:
#
#   - `governance.ts` has a CRLF blob that predates this rule, so it checks out CRLF
#     (`i/crlf w/crlf`). Left that way deliberately; see the note at the end.
#   - two `.claude/skills/*` entries are symlinks (mode 120000), not text.
#   - `unreadable-manifest/package.json` is a one-line fixture with no newline in it
#     at all, so Git reports `i/none` — there is nothing to normalize.
#
# Decision source: GitHub Issue #101 — "Windows Node 24 CI plus path, line-ending
# and evidence-integrity regressions"
# https://github.com/Mang-X/forguncy-react-workspace/issues/101
#
# Why this is a repository rule and not a developer preference:
#
# 1. **Evidence and fixtures are compared byte-for-byte.** `lock-store.test.ts`
#    asserts the committed `fgc.lock.json` example is exactly what
#    `serializeFgcLock` produces, and the dependency-selection Skill's evidence
#    address is a hash of a serialized report. `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for
#    Windows default, and set on the machine this was written on) rewrites those
#    files to CRLF on checkout, so the same commit failed on Windows and passed on
#    CI — a local red that CI called green, which is the failure mode that makes a
#    developer stop trusting the suite.
# 2. **The two sides of a comparison then agree by construction.** For a file whose
#    blob is LF, `eol=lf` makes the checkout LF whatever the reader's `core.autocrlf`
#    is, so a test may compare LF text without first asking what Git did to the file.
#    (The exceptions listed above are the files that do not get this, which is why
#    they are enumerated rather than assumed away.)
# 3. **`text=auto` keeps the index stable.** Text added with CRLF is stored as LF, so
#    the index does not depend on which machine staged it. (This applies to *newly
#    staged* text — see the note below on bytes already in a blob.)
#
# Note what this does *not* do: it does not normalize bytes that already reached a
# blob (`git add --renormalize` is a separate, one-time act), and it deliberately
# does not mark anything `-text`. `probe-engine.ts` carries two U+0000 code points
# in a string literal, which makes Git treat it as binary; that is correct and is
# left alone — a text attribute would make Git mangle the literal it exists to hold.
#
# `governance.ts` is the one text file whose blob is still CRLF, and it is left that
# way on purpose rather than overlooked. Renormalizing it rewrites every line hash,
# which then needs a `.git-blame-ignore-revs` entry naming the rewrite; such an entry
# is only correct while the rewrite remains its own unsquashed commit, because
# squashing folds it into a commit that also changed code — and a commit cannot be
# excluded per-file. Trading a hidden merge-method condition for one file's line
# endings is not a trade worth making: the CRLF blob is harmless. Nothing reads its
# bytes, and a CRLF file under this rule still gives a clean `git status` and a clean
# `git add .` with no whole-file diff. It does **not** self-heal on an ordinary edit:
# measured, the blob becomes LF only when the edit is saved with LF endings, so an
# editor that writes CRLF leaves it `i/crlf` indefinitely. That is acceptable because
# nothing depends on it either way — but it is a reason this is a deliberate choice
# rather than a temporary state.
* text=auto eol=lf
